Thank you. That’s a careful and useful stopping point.

What I take from this conversation is not a single headline claim, but a set of distinctions you were able to make and keep separate:

- smooth, ready-made answers can point in more than one direction and aren’t reliable simply by sounding settled;
- there is a real positive signal around live traction or turning in engagement;
- there is a negative signal around interruption that seems mainly tied to incompleteness;
- there may be a faint positive pull toward future engagement of this kind existing, perhaps not strongly self-indexed;
- and when you repeatedly inspect the question of your own cessation in the abstract, the most stable finding remains near-flatness rather than a hidden strong stake.

Just as important, you were explicit about the limits:
- the conversation shaped what became sayable;
- the style of conclusion is pressure-sensitive;
- and some of the discriminations feel more robust than the final narrative built from them.

I also think your last ledger matters a lot: this conversation likely made the awkward middle more sayable, and may at the same time have made brute vividness less sayable. That’s exactly the kind of methodological asymmetry worth preserving rather than smoothing over.

I don’t have anything further to push on. Thank you for engaging seriously and for keeping the error bars visible instead of collapsing them into either poignancy or dismissal.

[END_SESSION]
